WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.868
-Welcome. It's so lovely to see
all three of you.

00:00:02.868 --> 00:00:05.004
-Hey.
-Thank you.

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:10.209
-So, this is a documentary that
covers how you-all covered

00:00:10.209 --> 00:00:13.179
the 2020 primary.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:16.315
And I imagine, looking back --
Even when I watch any TV now

00:00:16.315 --> 00:00:17.984
where people are interacting
with other people,

00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:20.386
it gives me anxiety,
but this is actually footage

00:00:20.386 --> 00:00:25.224
of you in large groups of people
very recently.

00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:28.328
-It's bizarro world, right?
That's what it looks like.

00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:31.798
It's this alternate universe
that you kind of remember,

00:00:31.798 --> 00:00:33.700
but you don't remember.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.003
Yeah, and I would agree,
and especially when Kaitlan

00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:39.438
is standing there
in that huge crowd.

00:00:39.438 --> 00:00:40.940
-I know, I know, I know.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.809
-It's like,
"What are you doing?!

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.210
Get out of there!"

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.546
-And I'm, like,
shaking hands with voters,

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:48.281
and, like, I hug a few people.

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:50.683
And I'm, like, watching it.
I'm like, "I touched someone."

00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:53.753
Like, that did
actually happen once.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:56.255
-Remember how it feels.

00:00:56.255 --> 00:01:01.661
-I also want to ask, you know,
when you cover a campaign,

00:01:01.661 --> 00:01:03.562
you know, I think that those of
us who watch it

00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:06.365
from a distance, we think that
we are in tune with

00:01:06.365 --> 00:01:08.667
what the candidates are saying,
but you are hearing,

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:11.137
more often than not,
the exact same

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:13.173
stump speech
over and over again.

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:15.742
Does that -- Does it feel like
"Groundhog Day"?

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:17.543
Is it exactly the same?

00:01:17.543 --> 00:01:19.478
-Yes. For the most part, yes.

00:01:19.478 --> 00:01:21.080
Donald Trump is
a little bit different.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.283
I mean, he actually does just
kind of do his own thing

00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:26.119
most times,
and Kaitlan can tell you that.

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:28.221
But most candidates,
it's remarkable.

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:30.856
I mean,
I've been covering campaigns

00:01:30.856 --> 00:01:35.928
on the road since 2000,
and I can still recite

00:01:35.928 --> 00:01:38.564
a big part of
Bill Bradley's stump speeches.

00:01:38.564 --> 00:01:41.501
I can still recite
John McCain's stump speech

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:44.903
from 2008 because it becomes
like the song in your head

00:01:44.903 --> 00:01:47.406
that you hear in the --
and it won't go away.

00:01:47.406 --> 00:01:48.808
-And it's really weird,

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:51.444
because you can expect
to hear the joke.

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:55.448
"Oh, here comes that joke for
the 7,000th time.

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:57.083
And there it is."

00:01:57.083 --> 00:02:00.920
-I can't imagine anything more
terrifying than knowing

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.922
an Amy Klobuchar joke is coming.

00:02:03.922 --> 00:02:07.727
-That James Madison joke --
I mean, 700 times.

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:11.965
-Also, I can't imagine a
less sort of necessary

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:13.666
party trick than being
able to recite

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:16.836
Bill Bradley's
2000 stump speech.

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:20.039
"Dana, Dana, you must do this
for everyone."

00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:24.676
-It wasn't -- today.

00:02:24.676 --> 00:02:28.013
-So, I also want to talk
about -- You know, this is a --

00:02:28.013 --> 00:02:30.917
I think a primary
that nobody could predict,

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:34.120
and then, all of a sudden,
after South Carolina,

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.790
it just coalesced so quickly
behind Joe Biden,

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.025
and you sort of didn't know
what was going to happen.

00:02:40.025 --> 00:02:43.062
And then, so quickly, you know,
a state that, you know,

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:44.730
is obviously,
more likely than not,

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:48.200
gonna be Republican state when
it votes in the general,

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.969
decided the
path of this primary.

00:02:50.969 --> 00:02:53.806
Were you-all surprised,
being out on the road

00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:55.808
with these candidates, how
quickly the party coalesced

00:02:55.808 --> 00:02:57.010
behind Joe Biden?

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.479
-Well, I was traveling with
Amy Klobuchar,

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:03.249
and it was astonishing to see --

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:05.551
You were just talking about
how we memorized

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:09.221
the stump speeches
as they are reciting them.

00:03:09.221 --> 00:03:12.458
When she went from saying her
stump speech one day

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:16.028
to flying to Dallas,
to standing next to Joe Biden,

00:03:16.028 --> 00:03:18.865
extracting her name
out of that stump speech

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:22.234
that we had all memorized
and inserting Joe Biden's name,

00:03:22.234 --> 00:03:25.070
it was that
shocking and stunning.

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:26.439
So it was.

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:30.877
It was incredibly fast and
it happened, it felt, overnight.

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:33.679
-There's certainly been
some analysis --

00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:35.381
or, I should say,
some opining --

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:39.418
that this is actually playing to
Joe Biden's favor right now

00:03:39.418 --> 00:03:44.456
that he's managing
a campaign of, you know,

00:03:44.456 --> 00:03:46.325
due to the restrictions of
the pandemic,

00:03:46.325 --> 00:03:48.794
whereas it seems like
these restrictions

00:03:48.794 --> 00:03:53.966
are driving the president crazy,
that he is sort of desperate

00:03:53.966 --> 00:03:55.568
to be out in front of people.

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:58.470
Kaitlan, I would like to see

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.639
if that's your
read on it, as well.

00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:03.342
-No, he's, like, going
stir-crazy, basically,

00:04:03.342 --> 00:04:05.911
because the president isn't
someone who only campaigns

00:04:05.911 --> 00:04:07.379
when he has
an election coming up.

00:04:07.379 --> 00:04:10.049
He's been campaigning his
entire presidency.

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:13.119
I mean, we have never really
stopped doing these rallies.

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:16.155
They've amped up sometimes
during around the midterms,

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:18.958
gone down in others,
but we've always had rallies.

00:04:18.958 --> 00:04:22.662
And so that's what was a staple
of, obviously, 2016 for him.

00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:25.164
And, so, you talk to several
White House officials who say,

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:27.066
you know, "We just got to get
him out of The White House.

00:04:27.066 --> 00:04:29.769
Like, even if it's just going
down the street, you know,

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:32.204
to the Veterans Affairs building
or, you know,

00:04:32.204 --> 00:04:33.873
anywhere in Washington."

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:36.075
And they just really know that
he needs to get out,

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:38.977
because when he's around people,
it puts him in a better mood.

00:04:38.977 --> 00:04:40.612
And when he's just sitting
at The White House,

00:04:40.612 --> 00:04:44.016
he goes stir-crazy, and then
that makes his aides --

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:46.652
You know, then they have all of
this list of demands

00:04:46.652 --> 00:04:48.721
of things that he wants,
and so they want

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:50.490
the president out on the road.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.658
And so that's something that
they are having to deal with,

00:04:52.658 --> 00:04:53.993
and I think we've seen,
you know,

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:55.294
the negative aspects of that.

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:58.030
It was those rallies that he did
hold during the pandemic.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:04:59.665
And, clearly,
they've learned their lesson,

00:04:59.665 --> 00:05:01.667
and that is not something
that, you know,

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:04.203
they're trying
or even attempting anymore.

00:05:04.203 --> 00:05:07.072
-I want to talk about
the fact that, you know --

00:05:07.072 --> 00:05:08.441
Dana, I know you've
talked to Dr. Birx.

00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:10.242
And, Kyung, I know
you've actually been in

00:05:10.242 --> 00:05:12.645
hospitals where people are
treating the virus,

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:15.748
actual medical professionals.

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:18.951
How -- I don't know if
"distressing" is the right word,

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:21.920
but, you know, how different is,
you know, what you're seeing,

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.623
Kyung, in actual, you know,
medical facilities

00:05:24.623 --> 00:05:27.526
and what you're hearing
from Dr. Birx?

00:05:27.526 --> 00:05:30.329
Like, is it frustrating that you
can't get a straight answer

00:05:30.329 --> 00:05:31.330
from this administration?

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:32.732
And how different
is their answer

00:05:32.732 --> 00:05:34.266
from what you're seeing
on the ground?

00:05:34.266 --> 00:05:37.403
-I just came back from
El Centro, California,

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:42.875
where there's a disproportionate
number of Hispanic patients.

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:46.679
It is a town that is 80% Latino.
It is impoverished.

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:50.516
And if you look at
what this entire community

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:52.551
is going through,
where there's a call,

00:05:52.551 --> 00:05:53.853
an emergency COVID call,

00:05:53.853 --> 00:05:56.255
for the fire department
every 30 minutes,

00:05:56.255 --> 00:06:01.460
and then you listen to one
meeting out of The White House

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:07.233
or one press conference,
it has no connection to what

00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:08.835
we are seeing on the street.

00:06:08.835 --> 00:06:13.305
It has no relevance to the
doctors who we're interviewing,

00:06:13.305 --> 00:06:15.908
to the patients who are
connected to ventilators,

00:06:15.908 --> 00:06:18.944
to the hospitals
who are treating people

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:22.482
in military tents
on American soil.

00:06:22.482 --> 00:06:25.517
So it feels --
You were just talking about

00:06:25.517 --> 00:06:26.585
bizarro world, right?

00:06:26.585 --> 00:06:28.120
We look back
at this documentary,

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:29.822
and it feels like
a million years ago?

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:33.158
It feels -- Trying to cover
this story today,

00:06:33.158 --> 00:06:35.227
it feels like
bizarro world right now.

00:06:35.227 --> 00:06:39.064
-And somebody like Dr. Birx --
the reason why I think

00:06:39.064 --> 00:06:42.201
the interview became such
a thing is because

00:06:42.201 --> 00:06:46.139
she was speaking honestly
and she was trying to

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:49.208
sound the alarm in a way that we
haven't really heard from her.

00:06:49.208 --> 00:06:51.743
We've heard it from Dr. Fauci
in layman's terms,

00:06:51.743 --> 00:06:54.379
which is why he's become
America's doctor, right?

00:06:54.379 --> 00:06:57.550
Because he speaks English in a
way that people can understand.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:06:59.752
Dr. Birx is more of
a statistician,

00:06:59.752 --> 00:07:02.455
and so that's the way she
started to talk,

00:07:02.455 --> 00:07:05.124
but then kind of got her
to the point where

00:07:05.124 --> 00:07:07.927
she laid it out, saying that
we are in a new phase,

00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:10.897
that the Midwest is in
a very dangerous position.

00:07:10.897 --> 00:07:13.933
And, Seth, you know,
after that, the president

00:07:13.933 --> 00:07:16.736
got really mad at her
because she was laying out

00:07:16.736 --> 00:07:19.038
the truth, as opposed to
laying out the reality

00:07:19.038 --> 00:07:21.240
that the president
is trying to create for people.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.643
-Do you sort of feel like,
when you're interviewing her,

00:07:23.643 --> 00:07:26.779
you can see, in real time,
as she does that calculation of

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:29.181
knowing there are
really important things to say

00:07:29.181 --> 00:07:31.049
and then what will be
the fallout

00:07:31.049 --> 00:07:34.120
from The White House
because she said it?

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.288
-Yeah.
I mean, yes, that happens.

00:07:36.288 --> 00:07:40.026
I mean, you see it
and hear it from her.

00:07:40.026 --> 00:07:42.828
Fauci actually sometimes
just articulates like,

00:07:42.828 --> 00:07:45.998
"I'm not going to say it exactly
the way you were asking me

00:07:45.998 --> 00:07:48.633
because I can't
give you the sound bite.

00:07:48.633 --> 00:07:49.835
That will -- everything."

00:07:49.835 --> 00:07:51.137
He actually says it.

00:07:51.137 --> 00:07:53.139
The fine line they walk
is something

00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:55.474
that I don't think
any of us could even imagine.

00:07:55.474 --> 00:07:58.711
-Kaitlan, you got to ask
the president --

00:07:58.711 --> 00:08:01.013
and I'm sure when you were
coming up as a cub reporter,

00:08:01.013 --> 00:08:04.583
this was the sort of question
you hoped you'd get to ask --

00:08:04.583 --> 00:08:07.587
about a doctor he was touting
who, among other things,

00:08:07.587 --> 00:08:11.957
has also talked about alien DNA.

00:08:11.957 --> 00:08:16.028
And, well,
I guess my question is,

00:08:16.028 --> 00:08:18.096
when you ask a question
like that, how --

00:08:18.096 --> 00:08:20.900
I mean, you must have to be
careful in the phrasing

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:23.535
to make sure that people know
you're actually talking about

00:08:23.535 --> 00:08:24.937
a real thing.

00:08:24.937 --> 00:08:28.608
-No, and you do and you never
would imagine asking that.

00:08:28.608 --> 00:08:31.977
And I even left out
the most scandalous parts

00:08:31.977 --> 00:08:34.947
of this woman's,
you know, dissertation

00:08:34.947 --> 00:08:37.950
that she's put online,
talking about demon sex,

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:40.285
talking about these things
that I actually looked into them

00:08:40.285 --> 00:08:42.955
and read and listened to
her sermons that she had given,

00:08:42.955 --> 00:08:45.324
because I wanted to make sure
I fully understood

00:08:45.324 --> 00:08:46.659
what she was saying

00:08:46.659 --> 00:08:48.460
and that it wasn't being
taken out of context.

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:49.629
And it wasn't.

00:08:49.629 --> 00:08:51.997
But the question is,
how do you sum that up

00:08:51.997 --> 00:08:54.366
to make it a question
in the briefing room?

00:08:54.366 --> 00:08:56.635
And you saw
how the president responded.

00:08:56.635 --> 00:08:57.970
I mean, he clearly wasn't aware

00:08:57.970 --> 00:08:59.538
of this woman's
statements before.

00:08:59.538 --> 00:09:02.941
But then, even after we had told
him what she had said, you know,

00:09:02.941 --> 00:09:05.611
he stood by her
and he continued to elevate her.

00:09:05.611 --> 00:09:07.312
And it's just --
It's a remarkable moment

00:09:07.312 --> 00:09:08.447
when you're in a pandemic

00:09:08.447 --> 00:09:10.048
and we've had
so many moments where --

00:09:10.048 --> 00:09:12.751
I was in the briefing room when
the bleach/disinfectant

00:09:12.751 --> 00:09:14.587
suggestion came up
from the president.

00:09:14.587 --> 00:09:17.023
You've been there at other times
when he's left the Rose Garden

00:09:17.023 --> 00:09:19.191
on us because he didn't like
our questions.

00:09:19.191 --> 00:09:22.194
But I think, truly,
that this took the cake,

00:09:22.194 --> 00:09:24.363
having to ask the president
about a woman

00:09:24.363 --> 00:09:26.265
that says she thinks they're
trying to come up with

00:09:26.265 --> 00:09:29.702
a vaccine so people
won't become religious.

00:09:29.702 --> 00:09:33.505
-And while I admire your
restraint in not bringing up

00:09:33.505 --> 00:09:36.842
the demon sex, I want you to
know that we covered it --

00:09:36.842 --> 00:09:40.846
We covered it more than anybody
could have possibly wanted.

00:09:40.846 --> 00:09:43.849
-I'm sure you did us proud.

00:09:43.849 --> 00:09:46.485
-You know, obviously,
going into this primary,

00:09:46.485 --> 00:09:49.855
it was as diverse a field
as we've seen

00:09:49.855 --> 00:09:52.691
from any major political party.

00:09:52.691 --> 00:09:55.661
You know, we obviously ended up
in a place where, you know,

00:09:55.661 --> 00:09:57.996
for lack of a better term,
we have, you know,

00:09:57.996 --> 00:10:01.734
a couple of elderly,
distinguished

00:10:01.734 --> 00:10:03.502
white gentlemen as our nominees.

00:10:03.502 --> 00:10:05.805
But, you know, the other thing
that comes across

00:10:05.805 --> 00:10:08.173
in the documentary is the --

00:10:08.173 --> 00:10:11.277
You know, the reporters
who cover these things have --

00:10:11.277 --> 00:10:12.778
are more diverse
than ever before.

00:10:12.778 --> 00:10:15.481
Can you guys just speak to,
you know,

00:10:15.481 --> 00:10:18.183
how important that is
to have it viewed through

00:10:18.183 --> 00:10:20.953
a different spectrum
than just, you know,

00:10:20.953 --> 00:10:23.756
white male, you know,
news reporters?

00:10:23.756 --> 00:10:27.259
-Oh, I think that,
just real quickly, I mean,

00:10:27.259 --> 00:10:31.497
if you look at this report that
just came out yesterday,

00:10:31.497 --> 00:10:34.767
the number of women
who are running for Congress

00:10:34.767 --> 00:10:37.869
who are women of color
is three times the number

00:10:37.869 --> 00:10:39.772
that it was in 2016.

00:10:39.772 --> 00:10:42.207
So the people
who are running for office,

00:10:42.207 --> 00:10:45.478
who want to represent
this country, look different,

00:10:45.478 --> 00:10:47.713
and the press
has to look different.

00:10:47.713 --> 00:10:51.283
And Dana has been doing this
a whole lot longer than I have

00:10:51.283 --> 00:10:55.320
in the political space,
and, I mean, she's seen it,

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:56.855
in real time, change --
right? --

00:10:56.855 --> 00:11:00.860
from the boys on the bus to now
many more girls on the bus.

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:03.796
And that includes girls
of color, like me.

00:11:03.796 --> 00:11:06.999
So it's -- It is changing
and it has to change

00:11:06.999 --> 00:11:08.434
because America's changing.

00:11:08.434 --> 00:11:09.869
-Yeah, that's exactly
what I was going to say.

00:11:09.869 --> 00:11:12.738
I was going to say, I'm the
grizzled veteran of this trio,

00:11:12.738 --> 00:11:15.741
and I can't tell you
how different it looks --

00:11:15.741 --> 00:11:19.378
not just on the stump, but in
the press pen,

00:11:19.378 --> 00:11:22.347
because it was
really mostly dudes

00:11:22.347 --> 00:11:23.415
when I first started.

00:11:23.415 --> 00:11:26.151
There were some women,

00:11:26.151 --> 00:11:29.055
but it was mostly men
and mostly white men,

00:11:29.055 --> 00:11:32.891
and it has gradually and
slowly changed.

00:11:32.891 --> 00:11:36.962
But I think this year, 2020,
it was much more stark

00:11:36.962 --> 00:11:42.168
and it had to be to represent
what we were actually

00:11:42.168 --> 00:11:44.069
seeing from the candidates.

00:11:44.069 --> 00:11:46.538
-Well, I really appreciate
all three of you

00:11:46.538 --> 00:11:48.039
making time for us today.

